#include "globals.h"

extern int g_nSpyroSwingEulerPitchTarget;
extern int g_nCameraScriptStickRequestY;
extern int g_nCameraScriptStickRequestX;
extern int g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev0;
extern int g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev1;
extern int g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev2;
extern int g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum0;
extern int g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum1;
extern int g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum2;

/* 0x80049880: critically-damped spring that carries Spyro's three swing euler
   angles toward their targets and publishes them as byte angles. Per axis: the
   12-bit signed error drives a velocity accumulator (error*128 minus the
   current accumulator*16, scaled by 1/64), the accumulator advances the
   angle by 1/64, and the angle is republished as a byte euler (>> 4). */
/* PARKED 2026-08-11-3 at 81/80 insns (one insn long), structure-exact except
   the third guard. Re-derived from scratch; the 2026-07-25 permuter body is
   gone. What the original's block structure actually is (read off the branch
   targets, do not re-derive):
     stanza0 delta+guard, stanza0 spring, stanza1 delta+guard, stanza1 spring,
     stanza2 delta+guard, THEN the byte-0 and byte-1 publications, then the
     stanza2 spring, then byte 2.
   The two byte stores really do sit between the third guard and the third
   spring — sched1 cannot have moved them there (it is per-basic-block), so it
   is source order. Writing the three stanzas uniformly (byte published inside
   its own stanza) costs 124 masked mismatches; this order costs 84.

   A186 REFINEMENT (the load-bearing find): the original's first two guards each
   show `bnez; sll f,d,7` in the delay slot AND a second `sll f,d,7` after the
   arm. A186 says write the join insn ONCE — that is only reachable when the
   join insn's DEST is not the guarded variable's register. Written once, the
   shift is block-local at the join, local-alloc ties its dest to the dying `d`
   (both land in v1), and reorg then cannot copy it into the delay slot at all
   (the arm's `addiu d` would clobber the copy's input), so the slot gets a nop
   — three nops, +2 insns. Writing `f = d << 7;` BEFORE the guard and again
   INSIDE the arm makes `f` a cross-block allocno, breaks the tie, and both
   delay slots come out with the original's duplicate. That fix is in the body
   below (stanzas 0 and 1).

   SOLE RESIDUE (+1 insn): the third guard. The original fills its delay slot by
   MOVING the join's first insn (`sra v0,a2,4`, the byte-0 value — the arm does
   not clobber it, so no duplicate is needed) and computes `f = d << 7` once,
   seven insns later, in the load-delay bubble after the accum-2 load. Ours has
   sched1 put that shift first in the join block instead, so reorg duplicates it
   (+1). Measured, all identical at 81/84: shift after the accum load, shift
   fused into `(d<<7) - (a<<4)`, per-stanza `f` locals, byte stores written as
   `g_abSpyroSwingByteEuler[i]` (array-store form, A134) so the accum load
   cannot hoist over them, a volatile byte-0 store as a scheduling barrier, and
   the prev/accum accesses in array form rather than through the scalar aliases
   (that last one is worth recording separately: the two spellings are
   byte-identical here, so the `distinct symbols keep every access per-use
   absolute` note on the g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev0 aliases in symbol_addrs.txt
   does not bind this source shape).
   Class: F-sched straggler placement inside one basic block — the shift and the
   byte-0 sra are both priority-1 and the original's bubble choice is not
   source-reachable through statement order. Permuter-or-never. */
/* PARKED 2026-08-11-3 at 21/80 insns (73.8%), LENGTH- AND LAYOUT-EXACT (every
   branch lands at the original address). Re-derived from scratch; the
   2026-07-25 permuter body it replaces was structurally wrong.

   The block structure, read off the branch targets — do not re-derive:
   each stanza is `delta; guard; spring`, but the NEXT stanza's delta is
   computed BEFORE the current stanza's spring (the original software-pipelines
   it in source: the delta subu/andi sit in the join block of the previous
   guard), and the byte-0 and byte-1 publications sit between the THIRD guard
   and the third spring. Writing three uniform self-contained stanzas costs
   ~60 insns of scheduling difference; this order made the instruction sequence
   position-for-position identical.

   Four levers got it here, all measured:
     - A186 REFINEMENT: the first two guards show `sll f,d,7` in the delay slot
       AND again after the arm. Writing that join insn ONCE (what A186 says) is
       only reachable when its dest is not the guarded variable's register:
       written once it is block-local, local-alloc ties its dest to the dying
       `d`, both land in one register, and reorg then cannot copy it into the
       slot at all (the arm's `addiu d` would clobber the copy's input) — three
       nops, +2 insns, will not link. Writing `f = d << 7;` before the guard AND
       inside the arm makes `f` cross-block, breaks the tie, and both slots come
       out with the original's duplicate. The third guard must NOT use that form
       (it has no dup in the original).
     - the accumulator must be a PER-STANZA local (A138/A146): as one shared
       variable it is a cross-block allocno, conflicts with v0 and lands in a0,
       pushing `d` and `f` one register over each (-dg: 74 = 18 refs/31 insns,
       priority 2.32, allocated first). Three block-scoped locals let
   local-alloc give each v0, which is what the original has. 61 -> 39 insns.
     - `d` is ONE shared variable across all three stanzas (per-stanza `d`s put
       stanza 0 in v1); 26 -> 21.
     - the >>6 must be its own statement (`f = f >> 6; acc = acc + f;`, A167):
       written as `acc = acc + (f >> 6)` gcc makes a fresh temp instead of
       shifting in place. 29 -> 26.

   RESIDUE (21 insns, two classes):
   R1 (8 insns) the prev-1 / prev-2 held values swap a3 <-> t0. Both are 3-ref
      pseudos and the ranges differ by ONE insn (35 vs 34), so ours allocates
      prev2 first. Reachable only by an A176-style ref bump on prev1, and the
      only block that isolates its ref also carries two prev2 refs.
   R2 (13 insns) the stanza-2 tail: sched1 hoists the accum-2 load and the
      `d << 7` above the byte-1 store, where the original keeps the store first,
      and `f` then lands in a0 rather than the original's v1. Measured
      no-ops on R2: array-index byte stores (constant index folds to a fixed
      address, so A134's array-store barrier does not apply), a volatile accum-2
      load, a volatile byte-0 store, a separate stanza-2 `f` local (+1 insn,
      will not link), and the fused vs split `(d<<7) - (a<<4)` spelling.
   Class: F-sched/F1 pair. Permuter target — the logic and the layout are done.
 */
void SmoothSpyroByteEulerSpring(void) {
  int d;
  int f;
  int acc0;
  int acc1;
  int acc2;

  d = (g_nSpyroSwingEulerPitchTarget - g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev0) & 0xFFF;
  f = d << 7;
  if (d > 0x800) {
    d = d - 0x1000;
    f = d << 7;
  }

  d = (g_nCameraScriptStickRequestY - g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev1) & 0xFFF;
  acc0 = g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum0;
  f = f - (acc0 << 4);
  f = f >> 6;
  acc0 = acc0 + f;
  g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum0 = acc0;
  g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev0 = g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev0 + (acc0 >> 6);

  f = d << 7;
  if (d > 0x800) {
    d = d - 0x1000;
    f = d << 7;
  }

  d = (g_nCameraScriptStickRequestX - g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev2) & 0xFFF;
  acc1 = g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum1;
  f = f - (acc1 << 4);
  f = f >> 6;
  acc1 = acc1 + f;
  g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum1 = acc1;
  g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev1 = g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev1 + (acc1 >> 6);

  if (d > 0x800) {
    d = d - 0x1000;
  }
  g_abSpyroSwingByteEuler[0] = g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev0 >> 4;
  g_abSpyroSwingByteEuler[1] = g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev1 >> 4;
  acc2 = g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum2;
  f = (d << 7) - (acc2 << 4);
  f = f >> 6;
  acc2 = acc2 + f;
  g_nSpyroSwingEulerSpringAccum2 = acc2;
  g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev2 = g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev2 + (acc2 >> 6);
  g_abSpyroSwingByteEuler[2] = g_nSpyroSwingEulerPrev2 >> 4;
}
